From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 09:39:52 -0400
Subject: feat: sync the sidebar through a folder the user picks

Settings > Stedding > Spaces gets "Sync through a folder", off by default
(sidebar-file Sf7, Sf39). Set up... picks a folder and shows a sheet
before anything is written: this computer's name, the other computers
and the pairs, with Combine, Keep both and Cancel; a backup is taken
first (Sf8-Sf10). Each install then writes only its own
stedding-sidebar-<id>.json there, two seconds after the last change and
as quitting starts (Sf12), reads the others' files when the folder
changes, when a window comes to the front and every ten seconds while
one is in front (Sf13), and merges them field by field on a hybrid
clock, so a rename on one computer and a recolour on the other both
survive (Sf15, Sf16). A removal travels only from the user's own action
in a live window and only when the item is then in no window (Sf18,
Sf19); five or more removals, a third of the pins, fifty additions or a
Space deletion wait in Settings with Apply and Keep (Sf22, Sf23); a
change of a Space's own session and a move that would reload a loaded
page ask first (Sf24, Sf25). Peer files are parsed off the UI thread,
capped and refused whole when bad (Sf33); copies a sync tool makes are
named, never applied (Sf28); a clock far ahead is held (Sf27). The last
ten batches have Undo and five backups can be imported (Sf35). Stedding
makes no network request; the user's own tool moves the files (Sf36).

New SpaceModel and SpaceRegistry observer hooks report an unpin, a folder
leaving the pinned run and a deleted Space. The importer's id helpers and
unloaded-tab insert moved to sidebar_ids and unloaded_tab. The Mac's
folder usage strings are in InfoPlist.strings (Sf32).

Why: GitHub issue 20 asked for sync with no server and no account
(BACKLOG S-282, ADR 0029); export and import moved one Space at a time.
Removable when: not removable; it is product behaviour.
